    <h2 class="cta-block__heading">Generate Your Florida Quitclaim Deed Form</h2>
    <p class="cta-block__body">
      Free Florida-specific form with the correct two-witness blocks, notary acknowledgment, and
      documentary stamp tax guidance. Instant download.
    </p>
    <DeedForm />
  </div>

  <Disclaimer />

  <!-- What Florida Law Requires -->
  <h2>What Florida Law Actually Requires</h2>
  <p>
    Florida quitclaim deeds are governed by two key statutes. <strong>Fla. Stat. §689.01</strong> sets out
    the execution requirements — including the two-witness rule. <strong>Fla. Stat. §695.03</strong> governs
    the acknowledgment or proof needed for recording. A deed that doesn't comply with both isn't void
    between the parties, but it won't be accepted for recording, and an unrecorded deed leaves the
    transfer legally exposed.
  </p>
  <p>Under §689.01, execution requires:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The grantor's signature in the presence of two subscribing witnesses</li>
    <li>Both witnesses must sign the deed</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Under §695.03, to record the deed, its execution must be acknowledged or proved before a notary
    or other authorized officer.
  </p>

  <!-- Two-Witness Rule -->
  <h2>The Two-Witness Rule: Florida's Number One Deed Mistake</h2>
  <Callout type="warning" title="Two witnesses required — most national templates only include one">
    <p>
      Nearly every other state requires only a notary. Florida requires both — and the distinction
      matters. Florida §689.01 requires the grantor to sign in the presence of two subscribing
      witnesses. In practice, those witnesses must be physically present when the grantor signs and
      sign the deed themselves, in the grantor's presence.
    </p>
    <p>
      The witnesses do not need to be disinterested parties. A family member can serve as a witness.
      As a matter of conservative signing practice, the grantee (the person receiving the property)
      is generally not recommended as one of the two witnesses.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <!-- Notary Acknowledgment -->
  <h2>Notary Acknowledgment</h2>
  <p>
    The notary's role in Florida goes beyond witnessing. The notary must execute a formal
    <strong>acknowledgment block</strong> certifying that the grantor personally appeared, was known to
    the notary (or provided satisfactory evidence of identity), and acknowledged executing the deed.
  </p>
  <p>The notary block must include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>State of Florida and county</li>
    <li>The notary's statement of acknowledgment</li>
    <li>The notary's signature and printed name</li>
    <li>The notary's commission number and expiration date</li>
    <li>The notary seal</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Required Fields -->
  <h2>Required Fields on a Florida Quitclaim Deed Form</h2>
  <p>A properly drafted Florida quitclaim deed must include all of the following:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Grantor's Full Legal Name</strong> — use the exact legal name as it appears on the existing
      deed or title. If the grantor's name has changed, include both names.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Grantee's Full Legal Name</strong> — include the grantee's full legal name and, if there
      are multiple grantees, specify how title is held.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Property Legal Description</strong> — the parcel description from the property's existing
      deed. Do not use the street address alone. Find it on the existing deed, the county property
      appraiser's website, or a title insurance commitment.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Consideration</strong> — Florida requires a statement of consideration. For nominal or family
      transfers, "Ten Dollars and other good and valuable consideration" is standard.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Return Address for Recording</strong> — the county clerk mails the original deed back after
      recording. Include a name and mailing address in the upper left corner of the first page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Two Witness Signature Lines</strong> — each witness line must include a space for the
      witness's signature and printed name.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Notary Acknowledgment Block</strong> — must include the statutory acknowledgment language,
      notary signature, printed name, commission number, and expiration date.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Grantor's Signature</strong> — the grantor signs in the presence of both witnesses and the notary.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Documentary Stamp Tax -->
  <h2>Documentary Stamp Tax</h2>
  <p>
    Florida imposes a <strong>documentary stamp tax</strong> on deed transfers under
    <strong>Fla. Stat. § 201.02</strong>. Outside Miami-Dade County, the rate is
    <strong>$0.70 per $100, or portion thereof, of taxable consideration</strong>.
  </p>
  <ComparisonTable headers={docStampHeaders} rows={docStampRows} />
  <p>
    <strong>Important:</strong> The doc stamp tax is collected at recording. You pay it at the county
    clerk's office when you file the deed. Underpaying results in rejection; there is no grace period.
  </p>
  <p>
    Confirm the treatment under current Florida DOR guidance for the particular transaction before filing.
  </p>

  <!-- Step by Step -->
  <h2>How to Complete and Record a Florida Quitclaim Deed: Step by Step</h2>
  <div class="process-steps">
    <ProcessStep
      number={1}
      title="Obtain the legal description"
      description="Pull the existing deed from the county clerk's official records or use the county property appraiser's website. Do not use the street address."
    />
    <ProcessStep
      number={2}
      title="Identify all parties"
      description="Confirm full legal names for grantor and grantee. If there are multiple grantees, decide how title will be held (tenants in common, joint tenants with right of survivorship, or tenants by the entireties for married couples)."
    />
    <ProcessStep
      number={3}
      title="Complete the form"
      description="Fill in all required fields: grantor, grantee, legal description, consideration, and return address."
    />
    <ProcessStep
      number={4}
      title="Sign with two witnesses and a notary"
      description="The grantor signs in the presence of two subscribing witnesses, who sign as witnesses. The notary completes the acknowledgment block."
    />
    <ProcessStep
      number={5}
      title="Calculate the documentary stamp tax"
      description="Apply the three-part distinction: (a) true zero consideration with no encumbrance — documentary stamps may not apply; (b) stated nominal consideration — the $0.70 minimum may apply; (c) outstanding mortgage, lien, or encumbrance — use the $0.70-per-$100 rate on the encumbrance amount."
    />
    <ProcessStep
      number={6}
      title="Record with the county clerk"
      description="Bring the original signed deed, the correct recording fee, and the correct doc stamp tax to the clerk of court in the county where the property is located."
    />
    <ProcessStep
      number={7}
      title="Receive the recorded deed"
      description="After recording, the clerk stamps the deed with the official book, page, and instrument number, then mails the original back to the return address on the deed."
      isLast={true}
    />
  </div>

  <!-- Filing Checklist -->
  <FilingChecklist
    title="Florida Recording Checklist"
    items={checklistItems}
  />

  <!-- Where to Record -->
  <h2>Where to Record</h2>
  <p>
    File the deed with the <strong>Clerk of the Circuit Court</strong> in the county where the property
    is located, through the Official Records division. Do not file in the county where either party
    lives unless the property is also located there.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Miami-Dade County:</strong> Miami-Dade Clerk of Courts, Official Records Division</li>
    <li><strong>Orange County:</strong> Orange County Comptroller, Official Records</li>
    <li><strong>Hillsborough County:</strong> Hillsborough County Clerk of Circuit Court</li>
    <li><strong>Palm Beach County:</strong> Palm Beach County Clerk and Comptroller</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Common Mistakes -->
  <h2>Common Florida-Specific Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Missing the second witness.</strong> Florida requires two witnesses; one is not enough even
      when the notary also signs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Using the street address instead of the legal description.</strong> A street address alone is
      not an adequate substitute and can cause recording or title problems.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Filing in the wrong county.</strong> Record where the property is located, not where you live.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Defective notary acknowledgment.</strong> Florida requires specific statutory language in the
      acknowledgment block. A notary who only signs and affixes a seal produces a defective deed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Underestimating doc stamp tax when there is a mortgage.</strong> Under § 201.02, mortgages,
      liens, and encumbrances constitute consideration whether assumed or not.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Not recording at all.</strong> An unrecorded deed leaves the transfer legally exposed to
      subsequent creditors and purchasers who record without notice.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <!-- CitableAnswerBlock for SEO -->
  <CitableAnswerBlock
    question="What are the requirements for a Florida quitclaim deed form?"
    answer="A valid Florida quitclaim deed requires: the grantor's signature in the presence of two subscribing witnesses (Fla. Stat. §689.01); a formal notary acknowledgment block for recording (§695.03); the full legal description (not just the street address); and payment of documentary stamp tax at recording ($0.70 per $100 of consideration) under §201.02."
    source="Fla. Stat. §689.01; Fla. Stat. §695.03; Fla. Stat. §201.02 (Last Verified: August 2026)"
  />

  <FAQ items={faqItems} />

